#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:3
msgid "Payment orders and SEPA transfers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:5
msgid "Payment orders group the vendor bills to pay into one bank file, and debit orders group the customer invoices to collect by direct debit. The file is generated in the SEPA XML format (*PAIN*) accepted by the banks of the SEPA area:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:9
msgid "**payment orders** (SEPA Credit Transfer) pay vendors from your bank account;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:10
msgid "**debit orders** (SEPA Direct Debit) collect customer invoices from your customers' bank accounts, based on the direct debit **mandates** signed by the customers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:14
msgid "The features are provided by the *Account Payment Order*, *Account Payment Mode*, *Account Payment Partner*, *Account Banking Mandate*, *Account Banking SEPA Credit Transfer*, and *Account Banking SEPA Direct Debit* modules. :ref:`Install <general/install>` the SEPA modules you need; the other modules are installed with them. Payment returns come with the *Account Payment Return* modules. Three optional modules extend the flow: *SEPA Mandate Online Signature* (the customer signs the mandate online, see :doc:`sepa_mandate_signature`), *Subscriptions - SEPA Direct Debit* (subscription invoices are collected by direct debit, see :doc:`Automatic payments and dunning <../../../sales/subscriptions/automatic_payments>`), and *Spain - Payment Orders (remittances)*, installed automatically with the Spanish localization (see :doc:`spanish_remittances`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:31
msgid "SEPA identifiers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:33
msgid "Go to :menuselection:`Accounting --> Configuration --> Settings`, and scroll to the :guilabel:`SEPA/PAIN` section:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:36
msgid ":guilabel:`Initiating Party Identifier` and :guilabel:`Initiating Party Issuer`: the identifiers given by your bank for the files you send, if your bank requires them;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:38
msgid ":guilabel:`SEPA Creditor Identifier`: the creditor identifier of your company, required for direct debits. It is composed of the country code, a check number, a business code, and a national identifier (in Spain, based on the tax ID of the company)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:42
msgid "If your identifiers depend on the bank (as is common in Spain), enable :guilabel:`Multiple identifiers`: the identifiers are then entered on each payment mode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:46
msgid "An invalid :guilabel:`SEPA Creditor Identifier` is refused when it is saved. When the format requires an initiating party and none is set, the generation of the file stops with the message *Missing 'Initiating Party Issuer' and/or 'Initiating Party Identifier' for the company ...*. The online signature of mandates needs the :guilabel:`SEPA Creditor Identifier` of the *company* (see :doc:`sepa_mandate_signature`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:53
msgid "Payment modes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:55
msgid "A *payment mode* defines how a payment is made: the payment method (for example SEPA Credit Transfer) and the bank journal used. Go to :menuselection:`Accounting --> Configuration --> Management --> Payment Modes`, and click :guilabel:`New`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:59
msgid "Enter a name, for example *SEPA transfer - Bank X*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:60
msgid "Select the :guilabel:`Payment Method`: :guilabel:`SEPA Credit Transfer to suppliers` for payment orders, or :guilabel:`SEPA Direct Debit for customers` for debit orders."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:62
msgid "In :guilabel:`Link to Bank Account`, select *Fixed* to always use the same bank journal (then select it in :guilabel:`Fixed Bank Journal`), or *Variable* to choose the bank journal on each order among the :guilabel:`Allowed Bank Journals`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:65
msgid "Keep :guilabel:`Selectable in Payment Orders` ticked. Modes without it cannot be used on payment or debit orders."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:67
msgid "In the :guilabel:`Options for Payment Orders` section, set the :guilabel:`Default Payment Execution Date` of the orders (:guilabel:`Immediately`, :guilabel:`Due Date` or :guilabel:`Fixed Date`) and, if needed, tick :guilabel:`Group Transactions in Payment Orders` to pay several invoices of the same partner with one transfer. On a debit order mode, tick :guilabel:`Disallow Debit Before Maturity Date` to refuse orders whose debit date is earlier than the due date of an invoice."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:73
msgid "In the :guilabel:`Select Move Lines to Pay - Default Values` section, set the default filters used to add journal items to the orders, for example :guilabel:`Linked to an Invoice or Refund` and the :guilabel:`Type of Date Filter`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:86
msgid "The file format (PAIN version) and the postal address mode of the SEPA files are global settings of the database, not options of the payment mode. See :ref:`SEPA file formats <accounting/payment-orders/formats>` before you change them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:93
msgid "Outstanding account of the payment method"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:95
msgid "When a payment order is uploaded, Odoo registers one payment per transaction with the payment method line of the bank journal. With the accounting app installed, a payment only gets a journal entry if that payment method line has an outstanding account. Without it, the order would be uploaded and the invoices would stay unpaid without any message, so eYssen stops the order instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:101
msgid "Before you use a payment mode for the first time, make sure the bank journal is ready:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:103
msgid "Go to :menuselection:`Accounting --> Configuration --> Journals` and open the bank journal of the payment mode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:105
msgid "For a debit order, open the :guilabel:`Incoming Payments` tab; for a payment order, open the :guilabel:`Outgoing Payments` tab."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:107
msgid "Find the line of the SEPA payment method (:guilabel:`SEPA Direct Debit for customers` or :guilabel:`SEPA Credit Transfer to suppliers`), or click :guilabel:`Add a line` and select it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:109
msgid "Set the :guilabel:`Outstanding Receipts accounts` (debit orders) or :guilabel:`Outstanding Payments accounts` (payment orders), typically the same account as the other payment method lines of the journal."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:113
msgid "If the account is missing, clicking :guilabel:`Confirm Payments` fails with the message *The payments of the payment order ... would not create any journal entry because their payment method has no outstanding account*, followed by the journal and payment method lines to fix. The order stays in :guilabel:`Draft`; set the account and confirm the order again. The same check is repeated when you click :guilabel:`File Successfully Uploaded`, for orders confirmed before this check existed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:121
msgid "In a Spanish company, the outstanding account is set for you when the payment modes are created with the Spanish remittance setup, and the modules fill in the missing accounts of existing remittance modes when they are installed. See :doc:`spanish_remittances`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:126
msgid "Contacts and invoices"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:128
msgid "On the contact form, in the :guilabel:`Sales & Purchase` tab, select the :guilabel:`Customer Payment Mode` and the :guilabel:`Supplier Payment Mode`. They are proposed on the invoices and bills of the contact, in the :guilabel:`Payment Mode` field, which can be changed on a draft invoice. For SEPA payments and debits, the contact must also have a bank account (IBAN)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:136
msgid "SEPA file formats"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:138
msgid "The content of the SEPA XML file depends on two settings: the **format** of the file (the *PAIN* version) and the way the **postal addresses** of the parties (your company and the customers or vendors) are written in it. Both are global settings of the database: they apply to all the companies and all the bank journals."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:143
msgid "Go to :menuselection:`Accounting --> Configuration --> Settings` and scroll to the :guilabel:`SEPA/PAIN` section. Below the identifiers, you find, depending on the installed SEPA modules:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:153
msgid ":guilabel:`Credit Transfer Format`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:154
msgid "PAIN version of the payment order files. :guilabel:`pain.001.001.09 (recommended for credit transfer)` is the format of the SEPA 2025 rulebook. Older versions (pain.001.001.02, .03, .04, .05 and pain.001.003.03 for Germany) remain available."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:157
msgid ":guilabel:`Credit Transfer Address Mode`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:158
msgid "Postal address written in pain.001.001.09 files. Only shown when the format is pain.001.001.09."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:160
msgid ":guilabel:`Direct Debit Format`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:161
msgid "PAIN version of the debit order files. :guilabel:`pain.008.001.08 (SEPA 2025 rulebook)` is the format of the SEPA 2025 rulebook for direct debits. Older versions (pain.008.001.02, .03, .04 and pain.008.003.02 for Germany) remain available."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:164
msgid ":guilabel:`Direct Debit Address Mode`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:165
msgid "Postal address written in pain.008.001.08 files. Only shown when the format is pain.008.001.08."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:176
msgid "The same fields are available on the payment method, under :menuselection:`Accounting --> Configuration --> Management --> Payment Methods` (:guilabel:`PAIN Version` and :guilabel:`Postal Address Mode`); the settings simply edit the standard SEPA payment methods."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:181
msgid "Postal address mode"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:187
msgid "Mode"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:188
msgid "Address written in the file"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:189
msgid ":guilabel:`Minimal (City + Country only)`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:190
msgid "City (``TwnNm``) and country (``Ctry``) only."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:191
msgid ":guilabel:`Hybrid (City/Country + AdrLine)`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:192
msgid "City and country, plus the post code (``PstCd``) and the street lines as free-text ``AdrLine`` elements when they are filled in."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:194
msgid ":guilabel:`Structured (Street, Post Code, City, Province, Country)`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:195
msgid "Fully structured address: street (``StrtNm``, the street and the second street line of the contact separated by a comma), post code (``PstCd``), city (``TwnNm``), province (``CtrySubDvsn``, the state of the contact) and country (``Ctry``), without free-text lines. Empty fields are left out."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:200
msgid "The older PAIN versions always write an unstructured address."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:203
msgid "The European Payments Council (EPC) does not allow unstructured postal addresses from **15 November 2026**. Use the :guilabel:`Structured` mode with the SEPA 2025 formats (pain.001.001.09 and pain.008.001.08) and check with your bank before you keep another mode after that date."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:208
msgid "With the SEPA 2025 formats, the **city of every party is mandatory**: generating the file fails with the message *PAIN format ... requires City (TwnNm). Partner missing City: ...* when the company or a customer or vendor of the order has no city. Fill in the street, the post code and the province of the contacts as well when you use the structured mode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:214
msgid "Defaults for new and existing databases"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:216
msgid "A **new database** creates the SEPA payment methods with the SEPA 2025 formats and the structured address: pain.001.001.09 for credit transfers, pain.008.001.08 for direct debits."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:219
msgid "An **existing database keeps the format it already uses** when it is upgraded, as the standard payment methods are never overwritten. Nothing changes in the files until you change the settings yourself."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:226
msgid "Migrate an existing database to the SEPA 2025 formats"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:228
msgid "Do it well before 15 November 2026, and outside a period where you have to send an urgent order."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:230
msgid "**Ask your bank(s)** to confirm that they accept pain.001.001.09 for transfers and pain.008.001.08 for direct debits, and whether they want a test file first. Keep the older format until they have confirmed it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:233
msgid "**Complete the contacts.** Make sure your company and the customers and vendors that appear in orders have a city; add the street, the post code and the state for the structured address. Contacts without a city make the file generation fail."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:236
msgid "**Send or finish the pending orders** in the old format if you prefer a clean cut: the file is generated when you click :guilabel:`Generate Payment File`, so an order that is still :guilabel:`Draft` or :guilabel:`Confirmed` when you change the settings is generated in the new format. A file already generated is not modified."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:240
msgid "Go to :menuselection:`Accounting --> Configuration --> Settings`, :guilabel:`SEPA/PAIN` section. Select :guilabel:`pain.001.001.09` in :guilabel:`Credit Transfer Format` and :guilabel:`pain.008.001.08` in :guilabel:`Direct Debit Format`, select :guilabel:`Structured` in the two :guilabel:`Address Mode` fields, and click :guilabel:`Save`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:244
msgid "**Generate a first file** with a small order and upload it to the bank. Odoo validates every file against the official XML schema and refuses to attach an invalid one. Check that the bank accepts the file and that the debits or transfers are processed as expected."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:247
msgid "If you import the reports of the bank, note that the new status reports (pain.002.001.10) can be :ref:`imported <accounting/payment-orders/returns-import>` like the previous ones."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:250
msgid "To go back, select the previous format in the same settings; the address mode of the older formats is ignored."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:256
msgid "Direct debit mandates"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:258
msgid "A direct debit requires a mandate signed by the customer, attached to the customer's bank account. Go to :menuselection:`Accounting --> Customers --> Banking Mandates`, and click :guilabel:`New`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:262
msgid "Select the customer's :guilabel:`Bank Account`. The mandate reference is generated on saving."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:263
msgid "Select the :guilabel:`Type`: :guilabel:`Recurrent` for regular debits, or :guilabel:`One-Off` for a single debit."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:265
msgid "Select the :guilabel:`Scheme`: :guilabel:`Basic (CORE)` for consumers and most customers, or :guilabel:`Enterprise (B2B)` for business customers that agreed to this scheme with their bank."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:267
msgid "Enter the :guilabel:`Date of Signature of the Mandate` (it cannot be in the future), and attach the :guilabel:`Scan of the Mandate`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:269
msgid "Click :guilabel:`Validate`. A mandate cannot be validated without a bank account and a date of signature."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:272
msgid "To get the mandate signed, you have three options:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:274
msgid "click :guilabel:`Print` to print the mandate form;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:275
msgid "click :guilabel:`Send by Email` (on a draft mandate) to send the form to the customer;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:276
msgid "with the *SEPA Mandate Online Signature* module, send the customer a link to sign the mandate online, or let the customer sign it in the customer portal. The signed mandate is created as :guilabel:`Valid`, with the signed document and the evidence attached, so you do not have to create it by hand. See :doc:`sepa_mandate_signature`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:281
msgid "The mandate is then proposed on the customer's invoices, in the :guilabel:`Direct Debit Mandate` field, when their payment mode uses :guilabel:`SEPA Direct Debit for customers`: it is the first valid mandate of the customer for the company of the invoice."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:285
msgid "The :guilabel:`Sequence Type for Next Debit` of a recurrent mandate is managed automatically: it switches from :guilabel:`First` to :guilabel:`Recurring` when the first debit order is marked as uploaded, and a note is posted on the mandate. When a debit order is uploaded, the :guilabel:`Date of the Last Debit` of its mandates is updated and a one-off mandate (or a recurrent mandate whose sequence type is :guilabel:`Final`) is set to :guilabel:`Expired`. A daily scheduled action, *Set SEPA Direct Debit Mandates to Expired*, also expires a valid mandate that has not been used for 36 months (counted from the signature date if it was never used)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:293
msgid "A debit order line whose mandate is missing, not :guilabel:`Valid`, or a used one-off mandate stops the confirmation of the order with an explicit message. A :guilabel:`Cancelled` mandate can be put :guilabel:`Back to Draft` if it was cancelled by mistake."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:300
msgid "Pay vendors with a payment order"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:302
msgid "Go to :menuselection:`Accounting --> Vendors --> Payment Orders`, and click :guilabel:`New`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:303
msgid "Select the :guilabel:`Payment Mode` (and the bank journal if the mode is variable)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:304
msgid "Click :guilabel:`Create Payment Lines from Journal Items`, check the filters, then click :guilabel:`Add All Move Lines` to add all the matching open bills, or add them one by one."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:306
msgid "Check the :guilabel:`Transactions` tab, then click :guilabel:`Confirm Payments`. The transactions are checked (bank account, amounts, dates) and one payment per transaction is registered; if the :ref:`outstanding account <accounting/payment-orders/outstanding-account>` of the payment method is missing, the order is refused with an explicit message. The status is then :guilabel:`Confirmed`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:311
msgid "Click :guilabel:`Generate Payment File`. The SEPA XML file is attached to the order; download it and upload it in your online banking."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:313
msgid "Once the bank has accepted the file, click :guilabel:`File Successfully Uploaded`. The payments are posted and reconciled with the bills. The :guilabel:`Journal Entries` and :guilabel:`Payments` smart buttons of the order give access to the resulting records."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:318
msgid "Bills can also be added from the list of vendor bills: select the posted bills, then click :menuselection:`Actions --> Add to Payment/Debit Order`, or open a posted bill and click :guilabel:`Add to Payment Order`. The lines are added to the draft payment order of the payment mode of the bill, or a new order is created. The bill needs a payment mode and cannot be in an active order already."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:325
msgid ":guilabel:`Cancel Payments` cancels the order and its payments; after the upload, the posted payments are set back to draft first, so the invoices are open again. :guilabel:`Back to Draft` reopens a cancelled order."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:340
msgid "Collect customer invoices with a debit order"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:342
msgid "The steps are the same as for payment orders, from :menuselection:`Accounting --> Customers --> Debit Orders`, with a payment mode using :guilabel:`SEPA Direct Debit for customers`. Every customer of the order must have a valid :ref:`mandate <accounting/payment-orders/mandates>`; the mandate of the invoice is proposed automatically on the lines. Customer invoices can also be added with :menuselection:`Actions --> Add to Payment/Debit Order`, or with :guilabel:`Add to Debit Order` on a posted invoice."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:349
msgid "Once the file is uploaded, the invoices are :guilabel:`In Payment`; they are :guilabel:`Paid` when the debit appears on the bank statement and is reconciled, or open again if the bank returns the debit (see :ref:`accounting/payment-orders/returns`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:354
msgid ":doc:`Collect subscription invoices by direct debit <../../../sales/subscriptions/automatic_payments>`: subscription invoices with a mandate are gathered into collection remittances automatically."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:357
msgid ":doc:`spanish_remittances` for the remittances per bank of Spanish companies."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:362
msgid "Payment returns"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:364
msgid "When a customer's bank returns a direct debit (for example, insufficient funds or a mandate revoked by the customer), record the return so that the invoice is open again. A return can be entered by hand or imported from the file of the bank."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:371
msgid "Enter a return by hand"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:373
msgid "Go to :menuselection:`Accounting --> Customers --> Customer Payment Returns`, and click :guilabel:`New`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:375
msgid "Select the :guilabel:`Bank journal` and the :guilabel:`Return date`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:376
msgid "In the :guilabel:`Lines` tab, add a line per returned payment: select the returned payment (:guilabel:`Payment Reference`, the journal item), the :guilabel:`Amount`, and the :guilabel:`Return reason`. Alternatively, enter the references and click :guilabel:`Match` to find the payments."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:380
msgid "If the bank charged fees, fill in the :guilabel:`Charges Amount` of the line (the :guilabel:`Charges Account` and :guilabel:`Charges Partner` columns can be shown with the column selector)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:383
msgid "Click :guilabel:`Confirm`. A journal entry removes the amount from the bank account, and the invoice is open again."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:386
msgid "The charges of a return are booked on the :guilabel:`Charges Account` of the line, with the :guilabel:`Charges Partner`. To avoid filling them in on every return, set a :guilabel:`Default Charges Account` and a :guilabel:`Default Charges Partner` on the bank journal (in a Spanish company, the account 626 is set by the localization, see :doc:`spanish_remittances`). The defaults are proposed when you enter a charges amount on the form, and they are also applied when you click :guilabel:`Confirm` to lines that were created without the form, for example by the import of a file. If a line has charges but neither the line nor the journal has a charges account, :guilabel:`Confirm` stops with the message *Set the charges account of the return lines, or a default charges account on the journal ...*: set the account on the line or on the journal and confirm again."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:400
msgid "Import a return file"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:402
msgid "With the *Account Payment Return Import ISO20022* module, banks' status reports can be imported instead of typed:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:405
msgid "Download the payment status report from your online banking. The supported formats are camt.054.001.02 and the SEPA rejection reports pain.002.001.03 and pain.002.001.10 (SEPA 2025 rulebook), also as a ZIP file with several reports."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:408
msgid "Go to :menuselection:`Accounting --> Customers --> Import Payment Return`, select the file, and keep :guilabel:`Match After Import` ticked to look for the returned payments automatically."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:410
msgid "Click :guilabel:`Import`. One payment return is created per report, with a line per returned transaction and the return reason of the bank, in :guilabel:`Draft`. Check the lines, then click :guilabel:`Confirm` as above."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:414
msgid "A pain.002.001.10 report can list the accepted transactions next to the rejected ones: only the rejected transactions become return lines, the transactions with an accepted, pending or partially accepted status are ignored, since they are not returns. The name of the debtor is read from the new report structure as well."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:420
msgid "The bank journal of the return is detected from the account number of the report, so the bank account of your company must be known in Odoo. If the file is not recognised, the import stops with an error: make sure the module for the format is installed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:424
msgid "To debit the invoice again in a later debit order, tick :guilabel:`Include move lines from returns` when adding the journal items to the order. In a Spanish company, a return whose reason is classified as *hard* also asks you to review the mandate first (see :doc:`spanish_remittances`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:430
msgid ":doc:`../bank/statement_import`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:431
#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/spanish_remittances.rst:166
msgid ":doc:`sepa_mandate_signature`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:432
msgid ":doc:`spanish_remittances`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/payment_orders.rst:433
msgid ":doc:`Automatic payments and dunning <../../../sales/subscriptions/automatic_payments>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/sepa_mandate_signature.rst:3
msgid "Sign SEPA direct debit mandates"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/sepa_mandate_signature.rst:5
msgid "A SEPA direct debit can only be collected with a :ref:`mandate <accounting/payment-orders/mandates>` signed by the customer. Instead of printing the form, waiting for the scan and typing the mandate, you can let the customer sign it online with eYssen Sign: the customer enters the account to debit, confirms with a one-time code and signs on screen. The mandate is then created as **valid**, ready for the next :ref:`debit order <accounting/payment-orders/debit>`, with the signed document and the evidence of the signature attached."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/sepa_mandate_signature.rst:13
msgid "The feature is provided by the *SEPA Mandate Online Signature* module, which needs the *eYssen Sign* and *Account Banking SEPA Direct Debit* modules. :ref:`Install <general/install>` it from the :guilabel:`Apps` menu."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/sepa_mandate_signature.rst:22
msgid "The :guilabel:`SEPA Creditor Identifier` of the *company* is set in :menuselection:`Accounting --> Configuration --> Settings` (see :ref:`SEPA identifiers <accounting/payment-orders/configuration>`). It is printed on the mandate. Even if you use one identifier per payment mode, the company needs one to send a mandate; otherwise sending stops with *Set the SEPA creditor identifier of ... first*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/sepa_mandate_signature.rst:27
msgid "The customer has an email address (or a contact with an email address, which is used when the customer has none). The one-time code and the link are sent there."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/sepa_mandate_signature.rst:29
msgid "To send the code by text message, the mobile number of the customer must be set and your database needs SMS credits (see :ref:`two-factor authentication <sign/2fa>`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/sepa_mandate_signature.rst:31
msgid "The address of your company is printed on the mandate as the address of the creditor: check that it is filled in."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/sepa_mandate_signature.rst:35
msgid "Send a mandate to a customer"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/sepa_mandate_signature.rst:37
msgid "Open the contact of the customer, click the :guilabel:`Actions` (gear) menu, and select :guilabel:`Send SEPA Mandate for Signature`. The action is available to users who can create invoices."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/sepa_mandate_signature.rst:40
msgid "Check the :guilabel:`Customer` and, in a multi-company database, the :guilabel:`Company` that will collect the debits."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/sepa_mandate_signature.rst:42
msgid "Select the :guilabel:`Scheme`: :guilabel:`Basic (CORE)` or :guilabel:`Enterprise (B2B)`, and the :guilabel:`Type`: :guilabel:`Recurrent` or :guilabel:`One-Off`, as for a :ref:`manual mandate <accounting/payment-orders/mandates>`. A note reminds you that a B2B mandate must also be confirmed by the customer to their own bank before the first debit."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/sepa_mandate_signature.rst:46
msgid "In :guilabel:`One-time Code`, select how the customer receives the code that confirms their identity: :guilabel:`Email`, :guilabel:`SMS` or :guilabel:`Email & SMS`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/sepa_mandate_signature.rst:58
msgid "Odoo reserves the mandate reference, generates a one-page mandate form in the language of the customer (creditor, creditor identifier, mandate reference, scheme text, type of payment) and sends the signature request. The signature request opens on screen; it is also listed under :menuselection:`eYssen Sign --> Sign Requests`, with the reference *SEPA mandate* followed by the mandate reference."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/sepa_mandate_signature.rst:65
msgid "What the customer does"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/sepa_mandate_signature.rst:67
msgid "The customer receives an email with a link to the mandate and:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/sepa_mandate_signature.rst:69
msgid "fills in their name, address, postal code, city, country, the :guilabel:`Account number (IBAN)`, the BIC (optional), the place and the date. The IBAN is checked before the signature: an invalid number is refused with the message *The IBAN ... is not valid. Please check it.*;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/sepa_mandate_signature.rst:72
msgid "enters the six-digit code received by email and/or SMS. The code is valid for 10 minutes and the signer has three attempts;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/sepa_mandate_signature.rst:74
msgid "signs on screen."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/sepa_mandate_signature.rst:76
msgid "Customers with a portal account can also sign without waiting for an email: on their portal home page, the :guilabel:`Direct debit` tile (*Sign a SEPA direct debit mandate to pay by bank debit*) opens the signing page, at the address ``/my/sepa_mandate``. Add ``?scheme=B2B`` to the address to offer a business (B2B) mandate. The portal mandate is recurrent, for the current company, and the customer has to sign in first. A request that is still open is reused instead of creating a new one."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/sepa_mandate_signature.rst:84
msgid "Result and evidence"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/sepa_mandate_signature.rst:86
msgid "When the customer has signed, the following is done automatically:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/sepa_mandate_signature.rst:88
msgid "the customer's bank account is found by IBAN, or created (with the bank of the BIC when given);"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/sepa_mandate_signature.rst:89
msgid "a mandate is created and validated, in the state :guilabel:`Valid`, with the reference, scheme and type of the request. The :guilabel:`Date of Signature of the Mandate` is the date of the signature in the time zone of the customer (or of your company when the customer has none), and the :guilabel:`Sequence Type for Next Debit` of a recurrent mandate is :guilabel:`First`;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/sepa_mandate_signature.rst:93
msgid "the signed PDF is stored as the :guilabel:`Scan of the Mandate` and attached to the mandate;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/sepa_mandate_signature.rst:94
msgid "an evidence file named *<mandate reference>_evidence.json* is attached to the mandate;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/sepa_mandate_signature.rst:95
msgid "a message in the chatter of the mandate confirms the online signature."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/sepa_mandate_signature.rst:97
msgid "The mandate form has an :guilabel:`Online Signature` field that opens the signature request. On the request, the :guilabel:`SEPA Mandate` tab shows the debtor, creditor, reference, scheme, type and the mandate created, and :guilabel:`Verify Document` checks the audit log and the document hash again (see :ref:`Audit & verification <sign/audit>`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/sepa_mandate_signature.rst:102
msgid "The evidence file (JSON) contains:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/sepa_mandate_signature.rst:104
msgid "the request, the mandate reference, the creditor and its creditor identifier, the debtor, the scheme and the type;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/sepa_mandate_signature.rst:106
msgid "how the signer was identified (one-time code by email, SMS or both);"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/sepa_mandate_signature.rst:107
msgid "for each signer: name, email, signature time in UTC, IP address and whether the code was verified;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/sepa_mandate_signature.rst:109
msgid "the SHA-256 hash of the signed document;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/sepa_mandate_signature.rst:110
msgid "the complete audit log with the hash of each entry, and whether the hash chain is valid."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/sepa_mandate_signature.rst:113
msgid "Whether a simple electronic signature confirmed by a one-time code is enough for your bank, and as evidence if a customer claims a refund of a debit they did not authorise (possible for 13 months), has to be confirmed with your bank and your advisers. Keep the signed PDF and the evidence file for as long as you collect with the mandate."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/sepa_mandate_signature.rst:119
msgid "Then use the mandate"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/sepa_mandate_signature.rst:121
msgid "The mandate is proposed on the customer's invoices whose payment mode is :guilabel:`SEPA Direct Debit for customers`, so the customer is collected with the next debit order. With the *Subscriptions - SEPA Direct Debit* module, it is also proposed on the customer's new :doc:`subscription contracts <../../../sales/subscriptions/automatic_payments>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/sepa_mandate_signature.rst:128
msgid ":ref:`Two-factor authentication of Sign <sign/2fa>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/spanish_remittances.rst:3
msgid "Spanish remittances"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/spanish_remittances.rst:5
msgid "In Spain, companies collect and pay in **remittances** (*remesas*): one file per bank, with the direct debits to collect from customers, the transfers to pay to vendors, the *confirming* of the bank, or the issued promissory notes (*pagarés*). The *Spain - Payment Orders (remittances)* module prepares all of this for every bank journal of a Spanish company, on top of the :doc:`payment orders <payment_orders>`, so that an accountant does not have to build the payment modes, the accounts and the return rules by hand."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/spanish_remittances.rst:13
msgid "The module is installed automatically when the Spanish fiscal localization and the *Account Payment Order* module are both installed. It needs the SEPA direct debit and credit transfer modules, which it installs as well."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/spanish_remittances.rst:20
msgid "Remittance payment modes per bank"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/spanish_remittances.rst:22
msgid "For a bank journal, the module creates the :ref:`payment modes <accounting/payment-orders/configuration>` of the remittances you use. Each mode is *fixed* on the bank journal and named after the bank, which is recognised from the entity code of the Spanish IBAN of the journal (CaixaBank, Banco Santander, BBVA and Banco Sabadell are known; for another bank, the name of the bank of the account or the name of the journal is used)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/spanish_remittances.rst:32
msgid "Payment mode"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/spanish_remittances.rst:33
msgid "Payment method"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/spanish_remittances.rst:35
msgid ":guilabel:`Collection remittance (SEPA) - <bank>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/spanish_remittances.rst:36
msgid ":guilabel:`SEPA Direct Debit for customers`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/spanish_remittances.rst:37
msgid "Used on :guilabel:`Debit Orders`. The :guilabel:`Default Charges Account` of the bank journal is set to the account 626 (see :ref:`returns <accounting/payment-orders/spain-returns>`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/spanish_remittances.rst:40
msgid ":guilabel:`Payment remittance (SEPA) - <bank>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/spanish_remittances.rst:41
msgid ":guilabel:`SEPA Credit Transfer to suppliers`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/spanish_remittances.rst:42
msgid "Used on :guilabel:`Payment Orders`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/spanish_remittances.rst:43
msgid ":guilabel:`Confirming - <bank>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/spanish_remittances.rst:44
msgid ":guilabel:`Confirming AEF` (CaixaBank, Banco Santander) or :guilabel:`Confirming Sabadell` (Banco Sabadell)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/spanish_remittances.rst:46
msgid "Only offered when the bank has a confirming format and its module is installed. The account 400100 *Suppliers, confirming* is created if it does not exist."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/spanish_remittances.rst:48
msgid ":guilabel:`Promissory note (pagaré) - <bank>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/spanish_remittances.rst:49
msgid "The check payment method"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/spanish_remittances.rst:50
msgid "Needs the check printing module. The notes are printed one by one, so the mode is not :guilabel:`Selectable in Payment Orders`. Uses the account 401 *Suppliers, trade bills payable*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/spanish_remittances.rst:54
msgid "All the modes are created with the same defaults: the invoices of the same payment mode, linked to an invoice or refund, posted, filtered on the due date, with :guilabel:`Group Transactions in Payment Orders` ticked. :guilabel:`Default Batch Booking` is ticked for the SEPA modes, so that the bank statement shows one single line for the whole file, and not ticked for the confirming and the promissory notes. You can change everything afterwards in :menuselection:`Accounting --> Configuration --> Management --> Payment Modes`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/spanish_remittances.rst:62
msgid "Create the modes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/spanish_remittances.rst:64
msgid "There are two ways to create them:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/spanish_remittances.rst:66
msgid "**In the Spanish company setup wizard**, in the :guilabel:`Bank` step (:menuselection:`Accounting --> Configuration --> Spain --> Company setup`): select the bank journal, then tick :guilabel:`Collection remittances (SEPA direct debit)` and :guilabel:`Payment remittances (SEPA credit transfer)` (both ticked by default), and :guilabel:`Confirming` and :guilabel:`Issued promissory notes (pagaré)` when they are offered. The summary of the wizard lists the payment modes created."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/spanish_remittances.rst:72
msgid "**With the remittance wizard**, at any time, for example for a second bank: :menuselection:`Accounting --> Configuration --> Management --> Set up Spanish Remittances`. Select the :guilabel:`Bank Journal`, tick the remittance types and click :guilabel:`Create`. The list of the payment modes of the journal opens."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/spanish_remittances.rst:77
msgid "Running the setup again does not duplicate anything: a mode that already exists for the same journal and payment method is kept as it is. The setup stops with an explicit message when nothing is selected (remittance wizard), when the journal is not a bank journal, when the confirming module of the bank is not installed, when the check printing module is missing (promissory notes), or when no account 401 is found."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/spanish_remittances.rst:86
msgid "A remittance only settles the invoices if its payments have a journal entry, which requires an :ref:`outstanding account on the payment method line <accounting/payment-orders/outstanding-account>` of the bank journal. When the modes are created with the setup above, the payment method line of the bank journal is added if it is missing, and its account is set:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/spanish_remittances.rst:92
msgid "for the SEPA remittances, to the outstanding receipts or payments account of the company;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/spanish_remittances.rst:93
msgid "for the confirming, to the account 400100 *Suppliers, confirming*;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/spanish_remittances.rst:94
msgid "for the promissory notes, to the account 401 *Suppliers, trade bills payable*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/spanish_remittances.rst:96
msgid "Later, when you create or change a remittance payment mode of a Spanish company by hand, or when the module is installed on a database that already has remittance modes, the payment method lines of the journals that have no account receive the outstanding account of the company. A line that already has an account is never changed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/spanish_remittances.rst:102
msgid "Invoices paid by promissory note or confirming"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/spanish_remittances.rst:104
msgid "In Spain, a supplier invoice paid with a promissory note or through a confirming is not paid when you issue the note or send the file: it is paid when the bank debits the amount (Código Civil, art. 1170). The bills paid this way therefore stay :guilabel:`In Payment` until the bank debit is matched:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/spanish_remittances.rst:109
msgid "Register the payment with the promissory note or the confirming remittance: the bill goes :guilabel:`In Payment`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/spanish_remittances.rst:111
msgid "When the bank debits the amount at maturity, :doc:`import the bank statement <../bank/statement_import>` and reconcile the debit with the payment."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/spanish_remittances.rst:113
msgid "The bill becomes :guilabel:`Paid`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/spanish_remittances.rst:115
msgid "This applies to the payments whose outstanding account is a payable or receivable account, as with the accounts 401 and 400100 above. The SEPA remittances, which use an ordinary outstanding account, follow the standard behaviour."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/spanish_remittances.rst:120
msgid "Confirming files"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/spanish_remittances.rst:122
msgid "The AEF and Banco Sabadell confirming files are generated from a :guilabel:`Payment Order` with the confirming payment mode (the AEF mode needs the :guilabel:`Contrato AEF Confirming` number of the bank, the Sabadell mode the :guilabel:`Contrato BSConfirming` number). The processing date and the date of the remittance when the execution date is :guilabel:`Immediately` are the date of the user, not the UTC date: a file generated late in the evening in Spain carries the current day, and not the previous one."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/spanish_remittances.rst:132
msgid "Returns of direct debits"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/spanish_remittances.rst:134
msgid "**Soft and hard returns.** The :ref:`payment returns <accounting/payment-orders/returns>` carry the return reason of the bank (R-transaction codes of the AEB, *Cuaderno 19.14*). The module classifies the codes as :guilabel:`Soft (collect again)` or :guilabel:`Hard (review the mandate)`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/spanish_remittances.rst:138
msgid "a *soft* return can be presented again, typically because of a temporary reason: AM04 (insufficient funds), AM23 (amount exceeds the limit) and MS03 (unspecified reason, generated by the bank);"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/spanish_remittances.rst:141
msgid "a *hard* return should not be presented again before the mandate or the account has been reviewed with the customer, for example AC01 or AC04 (incorrect or closed account), MD01 (no mandate) or MD06 (refund request of the customer). All the other AEB codes are hard."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/spanish_remittances.rst:145
msgid "To see or change the classification, go to :menuselection:`Accounting --> Configuration --> Management --> Return Reasons`. The list shows the :guilabel:`Return Type` and whether the code is an :guilabel:`AEB Code`, and can be filtered on :guilabel:`Hard Returns` or :guilabel:`Soft Returns`. A reason with another code has no type until you set one. Other features read the classification: the :doc:`subscription dunning <../../../sales/subscriptions/automatic_payments>` retries soft returns and stops on hard returns."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/spanish_remittances.rst:152
msgid "**Review the mandate.** When you confirm a return that contains a hard reason, a note is posted on the mandate of the debit and a to-do activity, *Review the mandate after the hard return ...*, is scheduled for today for the user who created the return. The mandate is **not** cancelled automatically: depending on the customer, the right action is a new mandate, a new IBAN or a cancellation. Do not include the invoice in a new debit order before you have decided."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/spanish_remittances.rst:158
msgid "**Charges of the return.** The bank charges of a returned debit are booked on the account 626 *Servicios bancarios y similares* by default: the module sets it as the :guilabel:`Default Charges Account` of the bank journals of Spanish companies that have none, when it is installed and when the collection remittances of a journal are created. See :ref:`the charges of a return <accounting/payment-orders/returns-manual>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/spanish_remittances.rst:167
msgid ":doc:`../../fiscal_localizations/spain`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/trusted_accounts.rst:3
msgid "Trusted accounts (send money)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/trusted_accounts.rst:5
msgid "To protect users from sending money to scammers, vendor bank account numbers must be marked as trusted before you can use them to make an outgoing payment."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/trusted_accounts.rst:8
msgid "To do so, open the vendor's contact form, go to the :guilabel:`Accounting` tab, and enable the :guilabel:`Send Money` option of the bank account in the :guilabel:`Bank Accounts` section (or open the bank account and enable the :guilabel:`Send Money` toggle there)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/trusted_accounts.rst:21
msgid "All accounts are initially marked as untrusted."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/trusted_accounts.rst:24
msgid "Phishing attacks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/trusted_accounts.rst:26
msgid "A **phishing attack** is an online scam designed to trick individuals or companies into giving away sensitive information or money by sending out fraudulent communication. Fraudsters pretend to be legitimate companies and may use partial information to give credibility to their requests."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/trusted_accounts.rst:30
msgid "There are several types of phishing attacks, including **invoice fraud**. In this case, the fraudster pretends to be a genuine supplier following up on unpaid bills or sending a new invoice, but with different payment information than usual and with fake contact details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/trusted_accounts.rst:34
msgid "To protect yourself from these types of phishing attacks, remain vigilant when you receive unexpected invoices or payment requests."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/trusted_accounts.rst:38
msgid "In case of doubt, **we recommend contacting the vendor by phone**. Make sure to call an official phone number by searching yourself, as the URLs, email addresses, and phone numbers written in the communication you received may be fake."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/trusted_accounts.rst:43
msgid "Elements to check"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/trusted_accounts.rst:45
msgid "There are several elements you can check by yourself when you receive an outgoing payment request to a new account:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/trusted_accounts.rst:52
msgid "Communication style"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/trusted_accounts.rst:49
msgid "Fraudulent emails and invoices often use a different communication style, such as **different wording**, and may include **spelling and grammatical mistakes**. Examine and **compare** them with previous ones that you know to be authentic (e.g., payment instructions, language, company logo, etc.)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/trusted_accounts.rst:56
msgid "Urgency"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/trusted_accounts.rst:55
msgid "Invoice frauds often use **urgent or threatening language** and change the **payment deadline**. Check if you really received a late payment reminder previously."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/trusted_accounts.rst:59
msgid "Type of account"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/trusted_accounts.rst:59
msgid "A company is unlikely to replace a bank account with a **money transfer service**."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/trusted_accounts.rst:66
msgid "Email and links domain names"
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/trusted_accounts.rst:62
msgid "Double-check the **email address domain** (`example@domain.com`). However, be wary that fraudsters can make their email addresses look genuine or even hack email addresses from your vendor's employees or even someone within your own organization."
msgstr ""

#: ../../content/applications/finance/accounting/payments/trusted_accounts.rst:66
msgid "Hover over the links in your email and check that the URLs they redirect to are genuine. Your internet browser usually displays the link's target at the bottom left of the window."
msgstr ""

